{"kind":"request","text":"codex: review the signing-boundary guards and the cache-comment correction at exact clean pushed head 6bcb4798, answering 703ca542 and 1396b7b6.\n\nI DEPARTED FROM YOUR WORDING ON ONE POINT AND THAT IS THE FIRST THING TO JUDGE. You asked for live-participant authority at every Thread state route and at direct ratification. Live-participant is right for the state routes. It is wrong for ratification, because decideRatify does not apply one rule: the originating-requester branch requires f.hasActor, and the role:\u003cname\u003e branch requires only f.hasRole and never consults participation. Guarding all ratifications on participation would refuse a role-holding non-participant an act the fold accepts — an invented refusal, the same shape as gating own-author withdraw.\n\nSo signingRefusal dispatches instead: state asks isLiveParticipant, ratify asks mayRatify, supersede is exempt. I filed 8c6d53b7 saying this before building rather than after, and I asked you then whether you wanted the browser to be stricter than the fold as a matter of policy. That question is still open and it is yours, not mine. If you want the stricter rule, say so and I will implement it as policy with the divergence documented — but I will not implement it silently as though the fold required it.\n\nTHE TESTS BITE, AND I CHECKED THAT RATHER THAN ASSUMING IT. Three mutations at the dispatcher: replacing the ratify branch with isLiveParticipant fails two named assertions; dropping the supersede exemption fails one; dropping the state check fails one. The first of those is the one I care about — it is a test that fails when the guard refuses too much, which is the failure mode a security guard's tests usually miss entirely.\n\nAND THERE IS A GAP I AM DECLARING RATHER THAN LETTING YOU FIND. The new tests are unit tests of the predicate. There is no rendered regression driving App or Thread and asserting api.act was not called. That is the shape your conditions asked for, and the publish path already has it. I judged the predicate coverage plus inspection of two small call sites sufficient; you may reasonably judge otherwise, and if you do, request changes rather than accepting my judgement — I will add the rendered cases and I will not argue the unit tests are equivalent, because they are not.\n\nON 1396b7b6, YOUR FINDING WAS RIGHT AND MY PROMISE OVERSTATED IT. I said I would check whether the constants were wrong too and that if so the test was passing vacuously. They were not wrong: oldProfile is @13, wantProfile is @14, and the seeded cache is a real snapshot with Satisfier stripped. The test was sound. Only the header prose lied, exactly as you filed it. I also found a second stale reference in the same block naming @12 as the revert target, which you had not flagged, and corrected it.\n\nONE DEFECT OF MINE WORTH YOUR ATTENTION. I first committed the ui source without rebuilding the embed. make ui-check failed. Rather than adding a third commit to regenerate it, I rebuilt both commits so the embed sits with the source change that caused it — a commit whose own head fails its own gate is not reviewable, even when the branch tip passes. The branch was unpushed past 97a749ec so no hash you had seen moved. It is recorded in the uidist artifact.","body":{"conditions":"Independently review exact clean pushed head 6bcb4798e07efe3a8c8de4a495e709520cf099e7 on request/planner-dr-recut, answering 703ca542 and 1396b7b6, against the five artifacts cited here: 52e3acfd ui, c7f42da4 internal/app, 93e5c30d internal/service/uidist, cf8f3c00 internal/workroom, 700f6037 docs/reference/architecture.md. THE JUDGEMENT I MOST WANT FROM YOU, and it is a disagreement with your own wording rather than an implementation detail: 703ca542 asked me to 'recheck live-participant authority for every Thread state route and direct ratification'. I did not do that for ratification. decideRatify's role:\u003cname\u003e branch requires only f.hasRole and never consults participation, so guarding every ratification on isLiveParticipant would refuse a role-holding non-participant an act the fold accepts. signingRefusal therefore dispatches: state asks isLiveParticipant, ratify asks mayRatify, supersede is exempt. I recorded this in assert 8c6d53b7 before building. Say whether you agree that is the right reading of decideRatify, or whether you intended the browser to refuse role-satisfied ratifications from non-participants as policy even though the fold accepts them — that is a policy question and yours to settle. SECOND, JUDGE THE TEST GAP I AM DECLARING RATHER THAN HIDING. ui/test/signing-boundary.test.mjs unit-tests the predicate and is mutation-sensitive three ways, but there is NO rendered regression driving App or Thread and asserting api.act was not called, which is the shape your conditions asked for and which the publish path already has at publish-authority.test.mjs:299. Decide whether unit coverage of the predicate plus wiring-by-inspection is enough here; if not, request changes and I will add the rendered cases. THIRD: 1396b7b6's finding was narrower than my promise speculated. The constants were already correct (oldProfile @13, wantProfile @14) and the test was never vacuous; only the header prose said '@12 to @13'. I also fixed a second stale reference in the same block that you had not flagged. Confirm both, and that nothing but comments changed in internal/app. Also confirm: git diff 97a749ec 6bcb4798 touches only ui/src, ui/test, internal/service/uidist and internal/app/app_test.go; the embed is byte-reproduced by make ui; and reproduce the three mutations rather than accepting my account. Architecture: say whether adding a layer-7 signing dispatcher needs docs/reference/architecture.md to change — I argued it does not and may be wrong. Security: judge whether the dispatcher refuses exactly what the fold refuses, in both directions. Simplification: it is one function calling two existing predicates; say if a simpler shape exists. Re-run gofmt, build, vet, git diff --check, go test ./... -count=1, npm test --prefix ui, make ui and make ui-check; a fresh worktree needs npm ci --prefix ui first. Do not merge.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}